        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Paul the Apostle</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 5 CE – c. 64 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 5 CE (probably between 5 BCE and 5 CE) at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>; from a devout Jewish family of the tribe of Benjamin, a Pharisee, and by Acts a Roman citizen from birth</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 64 or 65 CE, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, after the Great Fire of Rome in July 64 and before the last year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s reign; early writers say he was martyred, and later tradition says he was beheaded</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Christian apostle and missionary to the gentiles, founder of churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the mid-40s to the mid-50s CE, and author of seven undisputed letters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>; also a tent-maker by trade, according to Acts</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">conversion on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>; three missionary journeys; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-jerusalem">Council of Jerusalem</a>; the letters to the Romans, Corinthians, Galatians and others; the argument that gentiles need not be circumcised; salvation by grace through faith; martyrdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></div>
        </div>
