    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Paul was a Pharisee who persecuted the early followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, then, after an experience on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, became the apostle who carried the message to non-Jewish communities around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</li><li>He argued that gentile converts did not need to be circumcised or follow the Mosaic law, and his letters, seven of which scholars accept as his own, are among the earliest Christian writings.</li><li>Scholars disagree whether he was a faithful Jew, as the Paul Within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> approach holds, or the second founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and Judaism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Gnostic groups have each read him differently.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>It has been estimated that Paul travelled over 12,000 kilometres by land and over 8,000 kilometres by sea on the journeys recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> alone.</p></aside>
    </section>

