    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5</span><span class="rf-text">Paul is born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, probably between 5 BCE and 5 CE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">31 to 36</span><span class="rf-text">After persecuting Christians he is converted on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, as Acts tells it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">46 to 49</span><span class="rf-text">With Barnabas he makes the first missionary journey, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, by the traditional dating</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">49</span><span class="rf-text">At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-jerusalem">Council of Jerusalem</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter">Peter</a>, James and John accept his mission to the gentiles</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">50 to 51</span><span class="rf-text">He reaches Corinth, where he founds the church and stays eighteen months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">56 to 57</span><span class="rf-text">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> he dictates the Epistle to the Romans</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">57</span><span class="rf-text">He goes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with a collection of money and is arrested in the Temple</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 60</span><span class="rf-text">He arrives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after a shipwreck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and spends two years under house arrest</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pharisees"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharisees.jpg" alt="Pharisees" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duccio_di_Buoninsegna_-_Christ_Accused_by_the_Pharisees_(detail)_-_WGA06802.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Duccio di Buoninsegna, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Paul, born Saul, was probably born between 5 BCE and 5 CE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, a trading city of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> known for its academy. His family was devout and Jewish; he called himself of the tribe of Benjamin, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> of the Hebrews and a Pharisee, born of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>. Acts says he was a Roman citizen by birth, and scholars largely accept this, though no source tells how his family gained it. Saul was perhaps named for the biblical King Saul, of the same tribe, while Paulus was a second name for use among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and Romans, not a result of his conversion as is commonly believed.</p><p>Acts says he worked as a tent-maker or leather worker and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to study under Gamaliel, though modern scholarship doubts he was preparing to become a scholar of the law. A sister's son is mentioned in Acts. His first language was probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, but he wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a>. Before his conversion he persecuted early Christians beyond measure, by his own account, and he took part in the martyrdom of Stephen. James Dunn suggests that he targeted the Greek-speaking Hellenists in Jerusalem, because of their anti-Temple attitude.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-peter.jpg" alt="Saint Peter" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter">Saint Peter</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Peter-Sinai_(6th_Century).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to Acts, Saul was on his way to arrest Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> when he saw a bright light, fell to the ground and heard a voice say, "Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?" and "I am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, whom you are persecuting." He was blind for three days until Ananias of Damascus laid hands on him, and he was then baptised and began at once to proclaim in the synagogues that Jesus is the Son of God. This account occurs only in Acts, not in his letters, where he says that God was pleased to reveal his son to him. His conversion has been dated to 31 to 36 CE.</p><p>Paul says he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, returned to Damascus and, three years after his conversion, visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, where he stayed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter">Peter</a> for fifteen days. He claimed a gospel received by revelation, not from men. Barnabas later brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, where followers of Jesus were first called Christians and from which the mission to the gentiles began. Around 45 to 46 Paul and Barnabas took famine relief from Antioch to Jerusalem. Acts says the believers had been scattered after the death of Stephen, and Antioch had a large Jewish community and many gentile God-fearers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/areopagus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/areopagus.jpg" alt="Areopagus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Areopagus_hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">O.Mustafin, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Acts arranges his travels into three missionary journeys. The first, with Barnabas, went from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and by the traditional dating took place in 46 to 49. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-jerusalem">Council of Jerusalem</a>, about 49, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter">Peter</a>, James and John, whom Paul calls the Pillars, accepted his mission to the gentiles on the question of circumcision. On the second journey, from late 49, he parted from Barnabas, took Silas and later Timothy, preached in Philippi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a>, and in Corinth founded a church where he stayed about eighteen months.</p><p>On the third journey he stayed almost three years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, probably working as a tent-maker, until riots over the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> forced him out. He then spent three months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 56 to 57, where scholars agree he dictated Romans. He also wrote to the churches of Corinth, Galatia, Philippi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and to Philemon. He held that Christ's death and resurrection reconciled sinners and gentiles with God, that salvation is by grace through faith, and that gentile converts need not be circumcised or follow Jewish dietary laws.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antioch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antioch.jpg" alt="Antioch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_on_the_Orontes_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UndercoverClassicist, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Paul met resistance from several sides. In Galatians he describes a public clash at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, where he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter">Peter</a> to his face over eating with gentile Christians, and even Barnabas sided with Peter. The Catholic Encyclopedia suggests Paul won the argument, but L. Michael White concludes that it was a failure after which Paul left Antioch for good. Paul and Barnabas later quarrelled over taking John Mark with them and separated.</p><p>Acts tells of opposition from some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the cities he visited, from the silversmiths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, and from a mob in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 57 who accused him of defiling the Temple by bringing gentiles into it. According to Acts a plot of 40 men against him was uncovered by his nephew. Scholars also dispute him. F. C. Baur argued that he was in violent opposition to the original twelve apostles, while Hyam Maccoby held that the Paul of Acts differs from the Paul of the letters.</p><p>Later critics charged that he corrupted or hijacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and that he differs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in message and practice, so that he was a second founder of the faith. Early Muslim writers said he altered the teaching of Jesus. Others, among them Daniel Boyarin, read him as a Jewish thinker who lived and died convinced he was a Jew. His remarks on women and on homosexuality are still debated, and most scholars regard 1 Timothy as not his.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero.jpg" alt="Nero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nero_Glyptothek_Munich_321.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 57 Paul arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> for the fifth and last time with a collection of money for the community there. James and the elders had warned him that he was said to teach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to abandon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, and he underwent a purification ritual. Even so he was seized by a mob and placed in chains by Roman soldiers. When he asserted his Roman citizenship he escaped flogging. He was sent under escort to Caesarea, where Felix kept him for two years until Porcius Festus succeeded him, and Paul then appealed to Caesar.</p><p>Acts tells of a shipwreck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, where the islanders showed him kindness, and an arrival at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> about 60. There he spent two years under house arrest, preaching from a rented home, with which Acts ends. It does not mention his death. His death is believed to have followed the Great Fire of Rome in July 64 and to have come before 68. Clement of Rome says he bore his testimony before the rulers and Ignatius says he was martyred. Eusebius says he was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s persecution, and Sulpicius Severus says he was beheaded. A tradition holds that he was beheaded where San Paolo alle Tre Fontane now stands. Some traditions say he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which scholars call conjectural.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/new-testament"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-testament.jpg" alt="New Testament" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ann_Arbor,_University_of_Michigan_Ms_P.Mich.inv._1570_(Papyrus_37)_recto_Matt_26,_19-37.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Paul's influence on Christian thinking has arguably been greater than that of any other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> author. 13 of its 27 books name him as author, and seven are almost universally accepted as his own; Ephesians, 1 and 2 Timothy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> are widely regarded as written in his name, and Colossians and 2 Thessalonians are debated. His letters contain the earliest mention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Lord's Supper</a> and gave later thinkers their material: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> drew on Romans for his work on grace, original sin and predestination, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s reading of Romans shaped sola fide and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and Karl Barth rewrote his commentary on Romans in 1922.</p><p>Marcion, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 144, held that Paul alone had rightly understood Christ. Gnostics prized his letters, and Valentinians claimed descent from Paul. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> took little interest in him until the 18th and 19th centuries, and Jewish writers such as Heinrich Graetz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-buber">Martin Buber</a> saw him as a barrier, others such as Claude Montefiore as a bridge. Muslim writers from al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi-abd-al-jabbar">Qadi Abd al-Jabbar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyya</a> depict him as having altered the teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p>His tomb is held to lie under the Basilica of Saint Paul Outside the Walls; a sarcophagus inscribed PAULO APOSTOLO MART was found there in 2002. He is the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, which keeps 10 February as a public holiday, and is remembered on 25 January and 29 June.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Paul, born Saul of Tarsus, was a Pharisee who persecuted the early Christians and then became the apostle to the gentiles, founding churches around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and writing letters that shaped Christian theology. He is traditionally held to have been martyred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>.</em></p>

